#!/bin/sh
# Stands in for bubblewrap (bwrap) on this desktop, and always says no.
#
# The desktop's programs load every picture, their own icons included, through a loader that first tries
# to lock itself into a bwrap sandbox. Inside this container bwrap cannot build one: Docker's AppArmor
# profile forbids the mounts it needs. The loader would take that failure for a broken picture, the
# desktop's bar and its session program would stop on their first icon, and the desktop would be started
# again and again. Told plainly that sandboxes of this kind are not available (the words below are the
# ones the loader, glycin, listens for), it loads pictures without one instead, as it does in any
# container that forbids them. The other way out, running the desktop without Docker's AppArmor profile,
# would take a guard away from everything in it, the browser included.
#
# Chromium does not use bwrap; its own sandbox is separate and stays on.
echo "bwrap: No permissions to create a new namespace (bubblewrap sandboxes are not available on this desktop)" >&2
exit 1
